---
// Local AI in Runebender. About 25 minutes: how a model becomes a folder
// beside your font, the hardware it runs on, the loop it serves, and the
// two routes into the editor.
// Companion to runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html and the demo repo.
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/end-runebender.jpg'
import grid from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-grid.png'
import nodes from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nodes.png'
import nufoEditing from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nufo-editing.png'
import outline from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-outline.png'
import titleRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/title-runebender.jpg'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Local AI (slides)"
  description="Talk slides on using local AI models with the Runebender font editor."
  running="Local AI"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a bismillah labeled for training: each letter’s ink inside a colored polygon."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Local AI</h1>
      <p class="t-head">Models that live<br />beside your font</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer 2026 · runebender.org</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>I make Runebender, a font editor in Rust. This talk is about running small models on your own machine, trained on your own drawings, from inside the editor.</p>
      <p>Everything shown is at runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html and in the demo repo.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Thesis" label="A model is a folder">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">A model is a folder.</p>
      <p class="t-display muted">Nothing leaves your machine.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Three files on disk. You point the editor at the folder. No account, no API key, no upload.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Why local ── -->

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Why local">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Why local</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Your drawings are your data. A model trained on them learns one
        design system, not a thousand scraped conventions.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Small on purpose. A few megabytes of weights, trained in minutes on a
        laptop GPU, that know your font and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Nothing downloads, nothing phones home. Models are directories you can
        open in Finder.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nufoEditing}
      alt="Runebender editing Arabic glyphs with a rendered preview."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Most font AI trains on scraped fonts and spends its capacity reconciling drawing conventions. We train on original work drawn on one grid. Consistency is signal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="The loop">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">The loop</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        You draw a core set well. The editor drafts the rest in your
        conventions. You grade and fix. Every accepted draft is new training
        data.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer judges and revises. Engineering diagnoses misses and
        builds tools. Neither replaces the other.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Draw and grade</td><td>A reference set in one master, marked green</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Train</td><td>The model learns from the green set and nothing else</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Draft</td><td>10 to 20 glyphs onto the machine grid, as a proposal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Review and fix</td><td>Judge, nudge, install, mark orange</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Grade</td><td>Corrected drafts go green and join the set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Build</td><td>Compile, proof, see it in use</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Diagnose</td><td>Name each recurring error; build a detector before training again</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Stage 5 is the arrow back to stage 1. The next finetune sees more green glyphs; the next batch needs fewer corrections.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Rules">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Rules every model respects</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        A draft is a proposal, never a direct edit. Install and Undo go through
        the ordinary proposal flow.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Only a human marks a glyph green. Machine drafts are orange until
        graded.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Drafts land on the 8-unit grid. Humans make the 2-unit optical
        corrections.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Measure before trusting. Every engine is scored against drawn pairs,
        and a plain mean shift is the baseline it has to beat.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">Nothing saves font files. The designer saves.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>These hold for the local models, for Claude over MCP, and for the chat panel. One safety contract, several routes in.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Hardware ── -->

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Hardware">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">What to run it on.</p>
      <p class="t-display muted">Memory decides.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>For people new to this. The chip matters less than how much memory the model can use and how fast that memory is.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Two numbers">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Two numbers</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Capacity: does the model fit. At 4 bits a model takes about 0.6 GB per
        billion parameters, plus room for the conversation.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Bandwidth: how fast it answers. Each new token reads the active
        weights once. Tokens per second is at most bandwidth divided by that
        size.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model</th><th>Parameters</th><th>File</th><th>Kind</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.5-4b</td><td>4B</td><td>2.9 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-20b</td><td>21B</td><td>12.1 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3.6B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gemma-4-26b-a4b</td><td>26B</td><td>17.0 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 4B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b</td><td>27B</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td><td>35B</td><td>22.4 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3B active</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>These are the chat models in my own ~/Runebender/models folder, 4-bit GGUF files. The file size is the memory the weights need.</p>
      <p>Leave room for the operating system and the context: on a 16 GB machine, stay under about 10 GB of model.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Dense and mixture of experts">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Dense and mixture of experts</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A dense model reads every weight for every token. A mixture of experts
        reads only a few experts per token.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        qwen3.6-35b-a3b holds 35B parameters and reads about 3B per token. It
        needs the memory of a large model and runs at the speed of a small
        one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>On a MacBook Pro, M4 Pro, 273 GB/s</th><th>Read per token</th><th>Ceiling</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b, dense</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>about 15 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 2 GB</td><td>about 130 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measured on AMD Strix Halo, 128 GB</th><th>Speed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Qwen3 30B-A3B, mixture of experts</td><td>about 100 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-120b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 55 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Llama 3.3 70B, dense</td><td>about 5 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The ceiling is bandwidth divided by bytes read. Real speed is lower. The point is the ratio: the mixture of experts is several times faster on the same machine.</p>
      <p>This is why large unified-memory machines are good for local AI now: the best open models are mixtures of experts.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Three kinds of machine">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Graphics card</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Fast memory, and little of it: 24 to 32 GB on one consumer card.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        NVIDIA’s CUDA runs everything, including training. A PC you can build
        and upgrade.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-8 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Unified memory</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The processor and the graphics share one large pool: 64 to 512 GB.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Slower per byte than a card, but large models fit. Macs, AMD Strix
        Halo, NVIDIA DGX Spark.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-9-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Many cards</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Several cards in one box, or a workstation chip with datacenter memory.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        For labs and teams. tinybox, RTX Pro 6000 builds, DGX Station.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Inference, running a model, works well on all three. Training mostly wants NVIDIA. Small models like the Runebender font models also train on Apple’s Metal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Desk machines up to 128 GB">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Desk machines, up to 128 GB</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Price</th><th>Good for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PC with a used RTX 3090</td><td>24 GB</td><td>936 GB/s</td><td>$700 to $1,050 per card</td><td>Most memory per dollar, CUDA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PC with an RTX 5090</td><td>32 GB</td><td>1,792 GB/s</td><td>$2,400 to $4,300 per card</td><td>Fastest consumer card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strix Halo mini PC</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>from about $2,000</td><td>120B mixtures of experts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Framework Desktop, Max+ 395</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>$3,449</td><td>The same chip, repairable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Studio, M5 Max</td><td>36 to 128 GB</td><td>460 to 614 GB/s</td><td>from $2,499</td><td>Quiet, MLX and llama.cpp</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NVIDIA DGX Spark</td><td>64 or 128 GB</td><td>273 GB/s</td><td>$3,999 at launch, now higher</td><td>CUDA in a small box, links in pairs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-credit">
        US prices, October 2026, from vendor pages and retail listings. Memory
        shortages moved them a lot this year.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Strix Halo is AMD’s Ryzen AI Max+ 395. GMKtec, HP, Beelink, Minisforum and Framework all sell it; the chip is the same in every box.</p>
      <p>The DGX Spark is the only one here with CUDA and 128 GB. Its bandwidth is the same as Strix Halo’s; you pay for the NVIDIA software, and for linking two units into 256 GB.</p>
      <p>A 64 GB DGX Spark from OEMs started at $4,999 in October 2026.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Bigger machines">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Bigger machines</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Price</th><th>Good for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Framework Desktop, PRO 495</td><td>192 GB</td><td>about 273 GB/s</td><td>$6,799 kit</td><td>Ships November 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Studio, M5 Ultra</td><td>96 to 512 GB</td><td>1.2 TB/s</td><td>from $5,499</td><td>The largest models in one quiet box</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PC with RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell</td><td>96 GB per card</td><td>1,792 GB/s</td><td>$9,000 to $16,000 per card</td><td>70B at high precision on one card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>tinybox green v2</td><td>4 × RTX 5090, 128 GB</td><td>1,792 GB/s per card</td><td>about $45,000</td><td>A ready-built multi-card box</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NVIDIA DGX Station, GB300</td><td>748 GB, 252 GB of it HBM</td><td>7.1 TB/s</td><td>$85,000 to $160,000</td><td>A datacenter GPU at a desk</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Pro</td><td></td><td></td><td>Discontinued March 2026</td><td>The Mac Studio replaced it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-credit">
        US prices, October 2026. The 512 GB M5 Ultra is expected above $10,000.
        DGX Station prices are set by OEMs.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The M5 Ultra has the same 1.2 TB/s at every memory size, so more memory buys capacity, not speed.</p>
      <p>tinybox is from tiny corp, the tinygrad company. Their red boxes use AMD cards and cost less.</p>
      <p>The DGX Station bandwidth is for its 252 GB of HBM. The other 496 GB is LPDDR5X at 396 GB/s.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="A Linux PC">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">A Linux PC</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The classic start is one used RTX 3090: 24 GB at 936 GB/s for about
        $1,000. Add a second for 48 GB. llama.cpp splits a model across cards.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The card is the computer. The rest only has to feed it.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Linux with CUDA is what every training tool tests first.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-7-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Part</th><th>What to look for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Graphics card</td><td>Memory first, then bandwidth</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power supply</td><td>1000 W or more for two cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motherboard</td><td>Room for two wide cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>System memory</td><td>64 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Disk</td><td>NVMe, 2 TB; models are large</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Processor</td><td>Any recent one</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>An RTX 3090 draws about 350 W. Two of them need a big power supply and good airflow.</p>
      <p>AMD cards work with llama.cpp through Vulkan and ROCm. Training tools support them less well.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="What I use">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">What I use</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        None of it is special hardware. The Runebender font models are one to
        a few dozen megabytes. Training them needs a graphics card, not a
        datacenter.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Job</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro</td><td>48 GB, 273 GB/s</td><td>Daily chat and coding, qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux PC, RTX 2060 SUPER</td><td>8 GB, 448 GB/s</td><td>Training, about 100 s per epoch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Steam Deck</td><td>16 GB shared</td><td>qwen3.5-4b at 18 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Linux PC trained the Nasta’liq model from the other talk. The Steam Deck runs Runebender and a chat model with the Vulkan build of llama.cpp.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Software that runs the model">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">The software</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A model file is not a program. A runtime loads it and serves it, often
        as a local web address that any app can call.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Runebender’s Chat panel talks to llama.cpp that way.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Runtime</th><th>What it is</th><th>Runs on</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>llama.cpp</td><td>GGUF files, the common format</td><td>Mac, NVIDIA, AMD, CPU</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MLX</td><td>Apple’s array library</td><td>Mac</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ollama, LM Studio</td><td>Friendly apps over those two</td><td>Mac, Windows, Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vLLM</td><td>Serves many users at once</td><td>NVIDIA, servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PyTorch, Candle</td><td>Training; font-ml uses Candle</td><td>NVIDIA, Mac</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Ollama or LM Studio is the easiest first step for most people. Download the app, pick a model, chat.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Which one">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Which one</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>To</th><th>Start with</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Try it today</td><td>The computer you have. A 4B model needs 3 GB.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Train small font models</td><td>Any NVIDIA card with 8 GB or more, on Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Run 30B models fast</td><td>A 24 to 32 GB NVIDIA card, or a Mac with 48 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Run 120B models quietly</td><td>128 GB of unified memory: Strix Halo, Mac Studio, DGX Spark</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Run the largest open models</td><td>Mac Studio, M5 Ultra, 256 to 512 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Serve a team, or train large models</td><td>RTX Pro 6000 builds, tinybox, DGX Station</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Start with what you have. Buy hardware when a real task is too slow, and buy for memory first.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Setup ── -->

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Two programs">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Two programs</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        <code>runebender</code> is the editor. <code>font-ml</code> runs and
        trains the models. Rust stable from rustup.rs builds both.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A mid-range computer is enough: an Apple Silicon laptop, a Linux PC
        with a gaming GPU, or a plain CPU with patience.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">cargo install --git https://github.com/fontgarden/runebender
cargo install --git https://github.com/eliheuer/font-ml \
    --features metal     # or cuda, or nothing for CPU</pre>
      <pre class="code">runebender --help
font-ml tasks</pre>
      <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/eliheuer/runebender-local-ai-demo
cd runebender-local-ai-demo
runebender fonts/Shapes.ufo</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The three font-ml lines are alternatives: Metal on macOS, CUDA on Linux with NVIDIA, CPU otherwise.</p>
      <p>font-ml tasks is authoritative. Only tasks marked ready are usable.</p>
      <p>The install takes about fifteen minutes of compiling. Do it before a workshop, not during.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Where models live">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Where models live</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        <code>~/Runebender/models/&lt;kind&gt;/</code>, a visible folder. Or
        beside the font in <code>MyFont.models/</code>, so the model travels
        with the font.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The kinds are different artifacts. A chat model does not draw shapes,
        and a shapes model does not talk.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Files</th><th>Comes from</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>shapes</td><td>config, weights, vocab, manifest</td><td>Local AI Shapes tool, from a UFO</td></tr>
          <tr><td>bolden</td><td>config, weights, vocab</td><td>font-ml run train, from two masters</td></tr>
          <tr><td>chat</td><td>model.gguf, tokenizer.json</td><td>Downloaded; the demo script fetches Qwen3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Nothing else is read. cat the config to see what you have.</p>
      <p>RUNEBENDER_MODELS overrides the root for one run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── The demos ── -->

  <Slide section="Demos" label="Demo 01: Local AI Shapes">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Draw from text</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Open any glyph. Hold the shapes button, pick Local AI Shapes. With no
        model yet, the panel offers Train From the Sample Shapes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        When it finishes, pick a shape, drag a box. The model fills it with a
        ghost. Enter keeps it, Tab asks for another, Esc drops it.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small muted">
        It knows only the shapes in the font and the words for them. Draw a
        heart, click Add This Font’s Shapes, and now it knows “heart”.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={outline}
      alt="Runebender editing an outline, with the right panel open."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A quick run takes about twelve minutes on a laptop GPU; thorough about thirty. The demo repo has get-base-model.sh with a checksum to skip the wait.</p>
      <p>That is the whole lesson: your drawings in, a model out, the model used from a tool.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demos" label="Demo 02: Bolden">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Bolden</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A model that moves points keeps a family interpolating. Regular in,
        Bold out, same point structure, on the 8-unit grid.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Weight debt block lists Bold glyphs still identical to Regular,
        drafts batches of 20 by offset or by model, and marks them orange.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        About half a second per glyph on Metal.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 demo-side">
      <Fig
        class="fig-right"
        src={grid}
        alt="Runebender’s glyph overview grid for a Latin font."
      />
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Virtua Bold</th><th>Owed</th><th>Drawn pairs</th><th>Offset vs model</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Latin</td><td>2</td><td>56</td><td>about even</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arabic</td><td>11</td><td>130</td><td>offset wins</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hebrew</td><td>71</td><td>5</td><td>offset wins</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Measured 2026-10-09 on Virtua Grotesk. Both engines barely beat the mean shift. Hebrew, with five drawn pairs, is the group to watch. Minimum before trusting an engine on a script: 20 green pairs, 5 held out.</p>
      <p>The score that matters is the distance between the draft and the outline the designer finally kept.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demos" label="Demo 03: a chat model">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">A chat model</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A Qwen3 GGUF from Hugging Face in the same folder layout. Ask it about
        the open font. It calls the same tools an agent would.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A small model cannot derive a stem from a point list. It calls the
        right tools in the right order every time. So the tools must return
        the number the question asks for.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Model</th><th>Time</th><th>Stem of H</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>font-ml chat (candle)</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>543 s</td><td>74. Wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>11 s</td><td>564. Wrong, bounds width</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 8B</td><td>24 s</td><td>Subtracted sidebearings. Wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server + analyze_glyph</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>5 s</td><td>100. Right</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Same question, same scratch copy of Virtua Grotesk, M4 Pro. Two lessons, both design decisions rather than model choices: the engine must be llama.cpp, and the tool has to do the measuring.</p>
      <p>Expect wrong answers at 0.6B. 4B is what the Chat panel was built with.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demos" label="Demo 04: Nodes">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Workflows are files</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The same train and bolden steps as a graph in the Nodes tab. Change
        <code>steps</code>, run again. The same file runs from the shell.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A graph run never saves or applies to the open font. Results are
        candidates until you install them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nodes}
      alt="Runebender’s Nodes tab with a workflow graph."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>External tasks declare their inputs so unchanged runs hit the cache. Model directories are hashed from their bytes, not trusted by manifest.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── The other route ── -->

  <Slide section="Agents" label="The other route: MCP">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Or bring your own agent</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        One command registers the live editor with Claude Code, Codex, Claude
        Desktop and Cursor. The agent talks to the open window and its unsaved
        state.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        45 tools. Read, measure, proof, propose exact point edits, fork
        experiments. Five workflows as prompts: review-glyph,
        compatibility-check, spacing-pass, kerning-pass, draft-weight.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small muted">
        Same contract: read first, proof the result, leave installing to the
        designer.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">runebender mcp --install</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind of AI</th><th>Reaches the font how</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Local models</td><td>font-ml tasks in the editor: shapes, bolden, brush</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the repo</td><td>Edits GLIF, plists, scripts, CI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the editor</td><td>runebender mcp --live, proposals and receipts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vision on proofs</td><td>Ranks drafts for attention; never grades</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local chat</td><td>The Chat panel, for people who will not install an agent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Every design app is growing an MCP. The differentiator is not the connection, it is the review loop (proof, compare, grade) and the safety contract (proposals, receipts, no saves).</p>
      <p>Nothing has to load into Runebender to help. The MCP is the adapter; any Python model in the lab can read the UFO and propose.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Agents" label="Draw only what the machine cannot infer">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack-lg">
      <p class="t-display">Draw what the machine cannot infer.</p>
      <p class="t-display muted">Grade the rest.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The end goal. A core set drawn well in one master; the editor drafts the rest in your conventions; the model gets better at your font the longer you work on it.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Live ── -->

  <Slide section="Live" label="Runebender">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={endRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a labeled bismillah and its preview."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-display">Runebender</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Switch to Runebender. Shapes.ufo, Local AI Shapes, drag a box, type star. Then the bolden on octagon, undo, chevron.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

